🧠 C Language Notes


🔹 1. Introduction to C
Developed by: Dennis Ritchie in 1972 at Bell Labs.

Type: Procedural, structured, compiled, middle-level language.

Uses: System programming, embedded systems, OS development.



🔹 2. Structure of a C Program
c
Copy
Edit
#include <stdio.h>  // Preprocessor directive

int main() {        // Main function
    printf("Hello, World!");  // Output
    return 0;       // Exit status
}


🔹 3. Basic Syntax
Semicolon (;): Ends a statement.

Comments:

Single line: // comment

Multi-line: /* comment */

Identifiers: Names for variables, functions.



🔹 4. Data Types
Type	Description	Example
int	Integer	int a = 10;
float	Floating-point number	float b = 3.14;
char	Character	char c = 'A';
double	Double-precision float	double d = 3.14159;
void	No value	Used in functions



🔹 5. Variables & Constants
c
Copy
Edit
int age = 25;
const float PI = 3.14;



🔹 6. Operators
Arithmetic: + - * / %

Relational: == != > < >= <=

Logical: && || !

Assignment: = += -= *= /=

Bitwise: & | ^ ~ << >>



🔹 7. Control Statements
if-else

c
Copy
Edit
if (a > b) {
    printf("A is greater");
} else {
    printf("B is greater");
}
switch-case

c
Copy
Edit
switch (choice) {
    case 1: printf("One"); break;
    default: printf("Other");
}




🔹 8. Loops
for

c
Copy
Edit
for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
    printf("%d", i);
}
while

c
Copy
Edit
int i = 0;
while (i < 5) {
    printf("%d", i);
    i++;
}
do-while

c
Copy
Edit
int i = 0;
do {
    printf("%d", i);
    i++;
} while (i < 5);



